1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,439
You have an essay to write, two chapters to read, and a quiz on Friday.

2
00:00:05,689 --> 00:00:09,232
Everything feels urgent, so it's hard to know where to start.

3
00:00:09,482 --> 00:00:13,067
You look at the whole list, and instead of starting, you put it off.

4
00:00:13,317 --> 00:00:16,518
If that sounds familiar, a simple plan can help.

5
00:00:17,768 --> 00:00:21,929
In this video, I'll show you one simple way to plan your study week.

6
00:00:22,179 --> 00:00:25,978
It has three steps: break it down, schedule it, and review it.

7
00:00:26,228 --> 00:00:29,451
You don't need special tools, or a perfect schedule.

8
00:00:29,701 --> 00:00:33,521
All you need is a notebook, or a calendar app you already use.

9
00:00:33,771 --> 00:00:37,228
Let's go through each step, using the same three tasks.

10
00:00:38,478 --> 00:00:41,701
Step one: break each big task into small steps.

11
00:00:41,951 --> 00:00:45,280
A small step is something you can finish in one sitting.

12
00:00:45,530 --> 00:00:49,755
It should start with a clear action word, like read, write, or check.

13
00:00:50,005 --> 00:00:52,502
Let's use the essay as an example.

14
00:00:52,752 --> 00:00:56,145
"Write the essay" is too big to start. So we split it.

15
00:00:56,395 --> 00:01:00,002
Choose a topic. Find three sources. Write an outline.

16
00:01:00,252 --> 00:01:04,456
Draft the introduction. Draft the body. Then edit and submit.

17
00:01:04,706 --> 00:01:08,782
Now every item is clear, and you know exactly what to do next.

18
00:01:09,032 --> 00:01:13,364
Notice that each step starts with a verb, and each one has a clear end.

19
00:01:13,614 --> 00:01:15,962
Do the same for the reading and the quiz.

20
00:01:16,212 --> 00:01:21,803
Read chapter four. Read chapter five. Review your notes. Try one practice quiz.

21
00:01:22,053 --> 00:01:26,236
Next to each step, write a rough time estimate. A guess is fine.

22
00:01:26,486 --> 00:01:30,135
Now we have ten small steps instead of three big tasks.

23
00:01:31,385 --> 00:01:34,245
Step two: put the small steps on a calendar.

24
00:01:34,495 --> 00:01:37,099
First, mark the time you actually have.

25
00:01:37,349 --> 00:01:43,132
In this example, there's about one hour on weekday evenings, and a longer block on Saturday.

26
00:01:43,382 --> 00:01:46,029
Your times will be different, and that's okay.

27
00:01:46,279 --> 00:01:50,035
Be honest here. Include classes, work, meals, and rest.

28
00:01:50,285 --> 00:01:54,126
Now place the steps. Start with anything that has a deadline.

29
00:01:54,376 --> 00:01:57,513
Each small step should fit inside one block of time.

30
00:01:57,763 --> 00:02:01,924
The quiz is on Friday, so the reading and review go early in the week.

31
00:02:02,174 --> 00:02:04,842
The essay steps fill the remaining slots.

32
00:02:05,092 --> 00:02:12,219
And leave one slot empty. Plans rarely go exactly as written, so a free slot gives you room to catch up.

33
00:02:12,469 --> 00:02:16,310
Now the week has a clear shape. You can see what to do each day.

34
00:02:17,560 --> 00:02:19,652
Step three: review and adjust.

35
00:02:19,902 --> 00:02:22,805
At the end of each day, check off what you finished.

36
00:02:23,055 --> 00:02:26,427
This takes about two minutes, and it shows you where you are.

37
00:02:26,677 --> 00:02:30,092
Say the outline took longer than you expected on Wednesday.

38
00:02:30,342 --> 00:02:33,351
That's not a failure. It's useful information.

39
00:02:33,601 --> 00:02:37,314
Move the unfinished step into your free slot, and keep going.

40
00:02:37,564 --> 00:02:42,067
If a step keeps getting pushed back, it may still be too big. Split it again.

41
00:02:42,317 --> 00:02:45,326
At the end of the week, ask yourself two questions.

42
00:02:45,576 --> 00:02:48,351
What worked? And what should I change next week?

43
00:02:48,601 --> 00:02:51,717
Use the answers to make next week's plan a little better.

44
00:02:52,967 --> 00:02:54,462
So, let's recap.

45
00:02:54,712 --> 00:02:57,465
One: break big tasks into small steps.

46
00:02:57,715 --> 00:03:00,660
Two: schedule them in the time you really have.

47
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:03,301
Three: review each day, and adjust.

48
00:03:03,551 --> 00:03:10,294
You don't have to get it perfect. A simple plan you actually use is better than a perfect plan you never start.

49
00:03:10,544 --> 00:03:14,577
Try it with just one task this week, and see how it works for you.

50
00:03:14,827 --> 00:03:16,428
Thanks for watching.
